1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,100
Listen to a short talk on architecture.

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:06,425
Before starting our tour of Monticello, I'd like to give you some historical facts

3
00:00:06,425 --> 00:00:09,100
that might help you appreciate what you see today even more.

4
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:14,500
Monticello was the very much loved home of Thomas Jefferson for over fifty years.

5
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:15,004
Jefferson,

6
00:00:15,004 --> 00:00:20,120
who was, of course, President, was also a great reader and language enthusiast.

7
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:22,791
He read widely on different subjects, including architecture.

8
00:00:22,791 --> 00:00:24,794
He wasn't formally trained in architecture,

9
00:00:24,794 --> 00:00:30,804
but as a result of his study and observation of other buildings, he was able to help design

10
00:00:30,804 --> 00:00:32,140
and build the house.

11
00:00:32,140 --> 00:00:38,200
He chose the site himself, naming the estate "Monticello," which means "little mountain" in Italian.

12
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:42,676
In fact, many of the ideas behind the design also came from the Italian

13
00:00:42,676 --> 00:00:43,635
architect Andrea Palladio,

14
00:00:43,635 --> 00:00:49,070
who lived in the sixteenth century and who had a great influence on the architecture of England.

15
00:00:49,070 --> 00:00:54,562
Jefferson, however, ignored one of Palladio's principles --- that is, not to build in

16
00:00:54,562 --> 00:00:55,829
a high place.

17
00:00:55,829 --> 00:01:01,320
Monticello's elevation made the transportation of what was needed at the house --- for

18
00:01:01,320 --> 00:01:03,010
example, food --- especially difficult.

19
00:01:03,010 --> 00:01:07,301
But the view from the estate would not be as spectacular if Jefferson had

20
00:01:07,301 --> 00:01:08,221
followed Palladio's advice;

21
00:01:08,221 --> 00:01:12,206
there really is no boundary between the house and the nature around it,

22
00:01:12,206 --> 00:01:16,497
and so Jefferson was able to look out on his beloved state of Virginia

23
00:01:16,497 --> 00:01:18,030
from his wonderful vantage point.

24
00:01:18,030 --> 00:01:20,220
Now we'll go on to Jefferson's library.
